speechtext:
The Prime Minister has made it perfectly clear, I think, that he does not intend to do anything in this matter. It is as well that the veterans of 1866 and 1870 should understand from the utterance of the Prime Minister, that, however he may have evaded the issue down to this time, he has made up his mind that he will do nothing for them, and /tie takes this opportunity and means of making that absolutely clear. The reference to the Minister of Militia, of course, is a mere evasion. The Prime Minister is allpowerful in this matter. There is nothing done in his cabinet which is not in entire accord with his view. He controls the whole situation, and he has controlled the situation which has 'brought us down to what has been announced by the hon. member for North Grey (Mr. Middlebro) and concurred in by the Prime Minister, namely, that as the result of cabinet deliberation the Minister of Militia says that nothing will be done. It makes it no stronger for the government to say that Sir John Macdonald should have done something, and would not do anything in reference to these veterans. The Prime .Minister must recognize-the country recognizes-that this defence of the country by the veterans was undertaken at a time when Canada was in a very different position from that which it occupies to-day. If the results to the country did not amply
justify our recognizing these heroes, there would be no one claiming from the government at this time that they should be recognized. Not reward, but patriotism, the Prime Minister says, should be the actuating motive of these men. I quite admit it. And of course patriotism and not desire for reward actuated these men when they went out in defence of the country. At that time they identified themselves in resources, in income, and even in their lives with the future of the country. They succeeded in defending the country and maintaining our position in Canada. And, the country having greatly prospered since, is it not a pity that a generous people should not in some way, having recognized many subsequent heroes, recognize also in some way the heroic action of these men? The Prime Minister speaks of the difficulty of discriminating-he would not reward them all. Those who got ready, those who took up arms, those who responded to the call but had not the . opportunity of fighting, he would not recognize. He would reward only those who were in active service. That may be a logical position, though I do not think so;
I would reward all those who came to the defence of the country at the call of duty. But if the Prime Minister-and he is allpowerful in this matter-has come to the conclusion with his government that they will discriminate, why do they not get at it? Why do they not do something? The Prime Minister says that those who were actually in action in the field of battle, and those only, he would reward; those who were only ready to go he would not reward. Well, why does he not do it? Fifteen years ago this matter was urged upon him. For forty-five years these men have been waiting and entitled to recognition. Why, then, does not the government do something? The Prime Minister has had the matter under that 'serious consideration' of his. When he was in the West last year, and during all the years since he came into power, he has had the matter 'under his serious consideration.' There is nothing to prevent him acting in the matter. Two or three years ago he said: I am in sympathy with you; I think you are en-entitled to something, and I would like to recognize you; I would do it if I could. I (will do it if I can and I will do it if the opposition will allow me. Has the opposition ever prevented him? Has not the opposition been ready, figuratively speaking, to pat him on the back? Have not they proposed resolutions asking him to do something? What is to prevent it? He knows. He could get up and tell us, but he does not do so? I know why he does nothing. It is because there is a certain element he is afraid of. Will he say now that it is not so? He knows that it is so.
